    "id": "news-1788441099165-8",
    "title": "Somali police unit seals central prison and court complex over unpaid wages",
    "original_title": "Ciidamo ka tirsan Asluubta oo xirtay Xabsiga Dhexe iyo Maxkamadda Gobolka Banaadir",
    "slug": "somali-police-unit-seals-central-prison-and-court-complex-over-unpaid-wages",
    "summary": "Troops from the Somali custodial corps sealed key access points to Mogadishu’s central prison and regional court on Thursday, disrupting movement after months without pay. Sporadic gunfire was reported as tensions escalated.",
    "content": "In Mogadishu on Thursday, members of the Somali custodial corps took control of the main entrances to the Central Prison and the Benadir Regional Court, halting both pedestrian and vehicular traffic. Witnesses reported occasional gunfire from the disgruntled officers, though no injuries were immediately confirmed. The lockdown came amid heightened tension in the area surrounding the prison, a key detention facility in the capital.\n\nThe officers involved are part of a unit that was officially registered nearly a year ago, yet they claim they have not received any salary since their enlistment. This prolonged period without compensation has fueled frustration among the ranks. The development follows a recent change in leadership, as the newly appointed commander of the custodial corps had begun implementing a new fingerprint-based identity verification system for all personnel, a move that some officers viewed as an additional burden without corresponding benefits.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this incident underscores the persistent challenges facing the country’s security sector, particularly the financial instability that can lead to sudden disruptions in essential services. Many diaspora members closely follow security news to assess risks for family visits and business trips, as well as to gauge the effectiveness of government reforms. This event also highlights the fragile nature of public institutions, where unpaid soldiers may take unilateral action that affects the rule of law.\n\nWhile the situation appears to have been contained without major escalation, it raises questions about the government's ability to manage its security forces and ensure timely compensation. The lack of pay for nearly a year points to systemic budgeting or management issues within the ministry of internal security. Observers note that similar incidents have occurred in other Somali security agencies, often leading to temporary standoffs until payments are released, but the ripple effects on public trust and operational readiness can be long-lasting.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/03/ciidamo-ka-tirsan-asluubta-oo-xirtay-xabsiga-dhexe-iyo-maxkamadda-gobolka-banaadir/",
